```ADD_PAPER
2201.12150v2
This survey reviews learning-curve methods for decision making in supervised machine learning, directly relevant to data-efficiency experiments such as comparing a model trained on 25% vs 100% of a training pool. It formalizes sample-wise and iteration-wise learning curves, and discusses parametric extrapolation models (notably the inverse power law) used to predict performance at larger training-set sizes from smaller subsamples. The authors contribute a three-axis framework categorising approaches by decision-making situation (data acquisition, early stopping, early discarding), technical question addressed, and data resources used. The survey reports that most sample-wise curves are well-behaved/convex in practice, and that while inverse-power-law extrapolation is widely used, no single model uniformly dominates across settings; both simple extrapolation and meta-learning approaches have complementary strengths. This is highly relevant to understanding how test accuracy and efficiency ratio vary with training sample size in logistic-regression experiments.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
