```ADD_PAPER
2412.07942v1
This paper proposes a percolation-theory model of natural data distributions to explain the universality of empirical neural scaling laws. It assumes context-dependent target functions and general-purpose learning mechanisms, then shows that at the percolation threshold the data distribution decomposes into a power-law (Zipf) distribution of discrete subtasks (quanta) corresponding to percolation clusters, while above threshold a dominant low-dimensional manifold structure emerges. This unifies prior theories: the subcritical quantum regime is associated with the quantization model (Michaud et al. 2024) and yields model scaling exponent alpha and data scaling exponent alpha/(1+alpha), while the supercritical regime corresponds to manifold approximation (Sharma & Kaplan 2022) with exponent c/D. For d>=6 the theory predicts alpha=1 and for ReLU networks c/D=1, giving model scaling L ~ N^{-1} and data scaling L ~ D^{-0.5}. Experiments trained nearest-neighbor regression models with a Bayesian prior on datasets generated from critical percolation clusters on a Bethe lattice with d=100, using 50 random seeds. Each cluster's individual scaling curves were fit with a plateau (P<m) followed by (P/m)^{-c/D}, and the measured model and data scaling curves were consistent with the theoretical predictions, although the fits were nonparametric approximations rather than neural networks. This work is relevant to data-efficiency research because it provides a theoretical account of how learning-curve scaling exponents and efficiency ratios relate to dataset structure, model capacity, and training sample count.
``` 
